% holding-updated: 2026-11-10
% holding-blocked: Have a Persian speaker check the romanised words and the drone (a koron second, a little flat, may be what Fairchild heard as F natural), and choose which verses a class sings.
% dossier-function: comic song: a suitor offers gifts (hot beetroot from the winter street-sellers, fireworks, a mattress, cigarettes) with a teasing refrain, "nā-nā-ī"
% dossier-elements: 4/4, slow but gay; each verse two short phrases ending "bi-ār-am?" (shall I bring?) on B, then the refrain falling from C to E; notes E F G A B C, ending on E, close to the dastgah Shur; no Western harmony (Fairchild avoided it on purpose)
% dossier-practice: a solo voice with the refrain answered loudly (forte); the band holds an open fifth on E with a frame-drum pattern of low dum and high tak
% dossier-accompaniment: Fairchild's piano part avoids Western harmony; Persian practice would be a voice with tombak or daf; the page's persian style (frame-drum dum and tak, a drone on E) stands in for it, not a rhythm a Persian musician has given us (conservative guess, 11 November 2026)
% dossier-lyrics: class version: the first line, "mi-khahi labu biaram?", sung twice, then the refrain; verse 1 line 2 and verses 2 to 4 left out (a second wife, a mattress, cigarettes); romanised words as Fairchild printed them, not yet checked by a Persian speaker (conservative guess, 11 November 2026)
% dossier-honesty: arranged: the melody and romanised words of verse 1 as Fairchild printed them; the frame-drum pattern and drone are ours and generic, not a tombak or daf rhythm a Persian musician has given us
% dossier-licence: public domain (Blair Fairchild, Twelve Persian Folk-Songs, London: Novello, 1904; Fairchild died 1933, Alma Strettell 1939; University of Rochester, Sibley Music Library scan, "This item is in the public domain"); read bar by bar from the 400 ppi scan on 2026-11-10
% dossier-respect: verse 1 line 2 jokes about bringing home a second wife; verse 2 line 2 is a bedroom joke (a new mattress by thine to-night); verses 3 and 4 offer cigarettes; the first line (hot beetroot) and the refrain are fine for any class, and the refrain is the fun part (corrected 11 November 2026, as the review found)
% dossier-review: a Persian culture bearer (a speaker and a musician of Persian folk song) before publishing
% source-url: https://imslp.org/wiki/12_Persian_Folk-Songs_(Fairchild,_Blair) IMSLP: Twelve Persian Folk-Songs (Novello, 1904), Sibley Library (Rochester) scan
X:1
T:Mīkhāhī labu?
T:Do you want hot beetroot? (Persian folk song)
C:traditional (Iran), collected by Blair Fairchild
O:Iran (Persia)
R:Persian folk song
S:Blair Fairchild (coll. and arr.), Twelve Persian Folk-Songs (London: Novello, 1904), no. 4, pp. 15-17; English words by Alma Strettell; University of Rochester scan
N:Fairchild: "The melodies themselves were written down as faithfully as possible just as they were sung to me in Persia." The verses keep the tune, moving a note to fit their words (verse 2 sings "qer-i-" on B B). The refrain is printed with its last E held over a further bar after verse 2; that ending is used here. Verse 2: mī-khā-hī mu-shak bi-ār-am, qer-i-to do-shak bi-ār-am? Verse 3: mī-khā-hī si-gār bi-ār-am, to-rā sar-i-kār bi-ār-am? Verse 4: di-dī keh si-gār ā-vord-am, to-rā sar-i-kār ā-vord-am? Strettell's English: Shall I bring thee some beets, my life? ... Shall I bring thee some fireworks bright? A new mattress by thine to-night? O my dear, my dearest dear!
M:4/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=88
